\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Cosine similarity between $e(\mathrm  {dt}{=}1)$ and $e(\mathrm  {dt}{=}2)$ across training configurations. Trained $\mathrm  {dt}$-embeddings (Walker and Cheetah) rapidly converge to near-antipodal representations ($\cos \in [{-}0.65, {-}0.90]$), while frozen and untrained embeddings remain near zero throughout training. The anti-correlation emerges within the first 25K steps, coinciding with the CIVO onset window (\S  \ref {sec:cosine_temporal}). This provides the representation-level smoking gun: trained gradient flow drives the embedding to encode $\mathrm  {dt}{=}1$ and $\mathrm  {dt}{=}2$ as maximally differentiated directions, creating the asymmetric latent space that underlies RSSM prediction bias.}}{28}{figure.caption.85}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cosine_similarity}{{15}{28}{Cosine similarity between $e(\dt {=}1)$ and $e(\dt {=}2)$ across training configurations. Trained $\dt $-embeddings (Walker and Cheetah) rapidly converge to near-antipodal representations ($\cos \in [{-}0.65, {-}0.90]$), while frozen and untrained embeddings remain near zero throughout training. The anti-correlation emerges within the first 25K steps, coinciding with the CIVO onset window (\S \ref {sec:cosine_temporal}). This provides the representation-level smoking gun: trained gradient flow drives the embedding to encode $\dt {=}1$ and $\dt {=}2$ as maximally differentiated directions, creating the asymmetric latent space that underlies RSSM prediction bias}{figure.caption.85}{}}
